WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.840
How do you handle harder

2
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.540
context-free pumping cases? Consider words

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.520
w hash t where w

4
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.060
must appear as a contiguous

5
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.020
substring of t. We will

6
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.240
use the witness a to

7
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.140
the p b to the

8
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.060
p, hash, a to the

9
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.260
p b to the p,

10
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.560
and organize every split around

11
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.340
the separator.

12
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.440
Assume this language is context-free

13
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.680
with pumping length p. Our

14
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.340
witness belongs because its left

15
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.120
word is exactly its right

16
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.960
word, and its length is

17
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.860
at least p. Take an

18
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.440
arbitrary legal u v x

19
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.240
y z split, remembering that

20
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.940
either v or y may

21
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:38.500
be empty.

22
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.440
If a pumped piece contains

23
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.420
the hash, choose exponent zero.

24
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.320
The only separator disappears, so

25
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.320
the result is not even

26
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.120
a correctly formed w hash

27
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.040
t word. This covers either

28
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.620
v or y containing the

29
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.440
marker, regardless of how the

30
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.400
other piece behaves.

31
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.940
If both nonempty pumped pieces

32
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.540
are entirely on the left,

33
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.480
choose exponent two. The left

34
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.600
word becomes strictly longer while

35
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.080
the right word remains length

36
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.380
two p. A longer word

37
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.880
cannot be a substring of

38
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.960
that unchanged shorter right word.

39
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.420
If both nonempty pumped pieces

40
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.920
are entirely on the right,

41
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.860
choose exponent zero. The right

42
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.700
word becomes strictly shorter than

43
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.260
the unchanged left word of

44
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.160
length two p. Again substringhood

45
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.000
is impossible, now because the

46
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.360
container became too short.

47
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.380
If exactly one piece is

48
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.340
nonempty, it lies on one

49
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.840
side or contains the hash.

50
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.580
The same left-only, right-only or

51
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.740
marker case applies. Do not

52
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.300
create an uncovered case just

53
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.740
because the lemma permits one

54
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.940
of its pumped pieces to

55
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:42.480
be epsilon.

56
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.220
The remaining possibility has the

57
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.420
hash inside x, with v

58
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.800
on the left and y

59
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.440
on the right. The bounded

60
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.020
window forces v to contain

61
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.900
only trailing b symbols from

62
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.160
the left word and y

63
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.800
only leading a symbols from

64
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.820
the right. Write v as

65
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.140
k copies of b and

66
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.840
y as j copies of

67
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.160
a, with k plus j

68
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:03.580
positive.

69
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.020
If k is positive, pump

70
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.240
up. The left word now

71
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.860
needs a contiguous run of

72
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.100
more than p b symbols,

73
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.980
while the right word still

74
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.800
has only p b symbols

75
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.780
in its final run. Extra

76
00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.000
leading a symbols on the

77
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.000
right cannot manufacture those missing

78
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.340
consecutive b symbols, so no

79
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.560
substring match exists.

80
00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.020
If k is zero, then

81
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.300
j is positive, so pump

82
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.320
down instead. The left word

83
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.640
remains length two p while

84
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.240
the right loses j symbols,

85
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.880
returning to the container-too-short contradiction.

86
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.540
Every legal split therefore fails

87
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.560
for a chosen exponent, proving

88
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.400
the language is not context-free.

89
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.320
For the duplicate-half language, the

90
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.520
lecture uses four long alternating

91
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.580
blocks and a separate midpoint

92
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.300
case analysis. Removing symbols moves

93
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.080
the midpoint, so saying that

94
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.920
only one original half changed

95
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.540
is not a sufficient argument

96
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.120
about the new halves. The

97
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.700
written study guide preserves those

98
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.720
cases; use the same careful

99
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.100
window discipline rather than reusing

100
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:06.620
a slogan.

101
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.660
Things to take note of.

102
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.060
Partition cases by what happens

103
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.680
to the unique marker and

104
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.140
by the sides containing the

105
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.660
nonempty pumped pieces. Length alone

106
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.120
handles same-side changes, while a

107
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.900
straddling window may require a

108
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.720
run-length argument and a different

109
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.840
exponent; every case must end

110
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.760
with explicit failed membership.
